  "htmlContent": "<h2>Welcome to Graph Connectivity</h2><p>In the world of computer science, we often represent complex systems as networks of interconnected points. Think of social networks, the internet, or a city's road map. These are all examples of <strong>graphs</strong>. But how do we analyze these structures? How do we determine if it's possible to get from one point to another, or find the most efficient route?</p><p>This chapter, <em>Graph Connectivity</em>, explores these very questions. We will learn the fundamental concepts and algorithms that allow us to understand how graphs are connected. We begin our journey with the most basic building blocks of graph traversal: paths and circuits.</p><h3>Paths and Circuits: The Basics of Traversal</h3><p>Imagine you are navigating a map. A route from a starting point to a destination is a <strong>path</strong>. If your route takes you back to where you started, you've completed a <strong>circuit</strong>. Let's define these terms more formally.</p><p>A <strong>path</strong> in a graph is a sequence of vertices where each adjacent pair in the sequence is connected by an edge. The <strong>length</strong> of a path is the number of edges it contains.</p><p>A <strong>simple path</strong> is a path that does not repeat any vertices. This is like taking a route where you never visit the same intersection twice.</p><blockquote><p><strong>Example: A Simple Path</strong></p><p>Consider the graph from our reference slide. The sequence of vertices <em>a, b, c, d, f</em> forms a simple path. It follows the edges (a,b), (b,c), (c,d), and (d,f). Since it uses 4 edges, its length is 4. No vertex is visited more than once.</p></blockquote><p>A <strong>circuit</strong> (also known as a <strong>cycle</strong>) is a path that starts and ends at the same vertex and has a length of at least one. A <strong>simple circuit</strong> is a circuit that does not repeat any vertices, except for the starting and ending vertex.</p><blockquote><p><strong>Example: A Circuit</strong></p><p>In the same graph, the sequence <em>a, b, c, d, a</em> forms a circuit. It starts at vertex 'a' and returns to vertex 'a'. Its length is also 4. This is a simple circuit because only the start/end vertex 'a' is repeated.</p></blockquote><h3>Key Definitions to Remember</h3><ul><li><strong>Path:</strong> A sequence of edges connecting a sequence of vertices.</li><li><strong>Simple Path:</strong> A path that does not contain a repeated vertex.</li><li><strong>Circuit:</strong> A path that begins and ends at the same vertex.</li><li><strong>Simple Circuit:</strong> A circuit with no repeated vertices, except for the first and last.</li><li><strong>Length:</strong> The number of edges in a path or circuit.</li></ul><h3>Summary</h3><p>In this lesson, we've defined the foundational concepts of paths and circuits. A path is a way to travel from one vertex to another, while a circuit is a round trip. Understanding the distinction between general paths/circuits and their 'simple' counterparts is crucial. These concepts are the essential vocabulary we'll need as we explore more complex connectivity problems, such as finding Euler and Hamilton paths in our next lesson.</p>",
  "pageTitle": "Introduction to Graph Connectivity",
  "shortSummary": "This lesson introduces the fundamental concepts of graph connectivity, defining and illustrating the key building blocks of graph traversal: paths and circuits."
